A fictional marketing team releases campaign summaries. Its release-dependency setting permits each validated summary to publish independently; West can be recovered later. The controller has reconciled all three IDs. What should it do at this release boundary?

Initial situation

Release the available campaign summaries. Genuine batch fields and result-type fragment:
{"processing_status":"ended","request_counts":{"processing":0,"succeeded":2,"errored":0,"canceled":0,"expired":1}}
{"custom_id":"campaign_north","result":{"type":"succeeded"}}
{"custom_id":"campaign_west","result":{"type":"expired"}}
{"custom_id":"campaign_south","result":{"type":"succeeded"}}
Result lines are deliberately abbreviated; succeeded messages are omitted. Application-owned ledger confirms both successful outputs passed validation and each custom_id maps to one immutable campaign input. No publication has occurred. The application releases the same campaign-summary package in both situations. The single release-dependency setting determines whether validated summaries may release separately or must wait for all campaign inputs. Existing successful summaries need no additional review.

The same marketing summary package now has its release dependency set to all three campaign inputs. West remains expired and no release has occurred. What should the controller do at this release boundary?

Only the release-dependency setting changes: the same summary package must now wait until all three campaigns have validated outputs. Results, review requirements and deliverable identity remain unchanged.

